This project demonstrates an end-to-end data pipeline that transforms a raw accounts receivable (AR) dataset into a structured, queryable SQL database — showcasing skills across Python data engineering, data quality, and analytical SQL. The dataset consists of 300 invoice records spanning multiple customers, currencies (USD, GBP, EUR), and source systems (SAP, QuickBooks, NetSuite, Oracle), simulating the kind of multi-system AR environment common in mid-to-large enterprise finance operations.
The Python pipeline, built in a Jupyter Notebook using pandas, handles the data preparation and enrichment phase. After ingesting the raw Excel file, the notebook engineers three new columns critical to AR analysis: open_amount_due (calculated as invoice amount minus amount paid), days_past_due (computed relative to a defined analysis date of January 1, 2025), and age_bucket (a categorical column that segments each invoice into standard aging tiers: Current, 1–30, 31–60, 61–90, 91–180, and 181–365 days). Data quality steps were applied throughout, including null handling for non-delinquent invoices, data type standardization, and conversion of float-based day counts to clean integers.
Once the dataset was fully transformed and validated, it was loaded directly into a SQL Server database (ARAgingDB) using SQLAlchemy and the pyodbc ODBC driver — completing a Python-to-SQL pipeline with a live database connection. This approach reflects real-world data engineering practice, where clean, enriched data is staged in a relational database for downstream consumption by analysts and reporting tools.
With the data in SQL Server, a series of analytical queries were written to surface key AR insights. These included a source system breakdown showing each platform's share of total AR (ranked highest to lowest), and a customer-level pivot table that spreads open balances across all age buckets with a total AR column — the standard format used in collections and finance reporting. Together, these queries demonstrate practical SQL skills including aggregation, conditional logic with CASE WHEN, subqueries, and GROUP BY with multi-column combinations.
This project highlights the full workflow a data analyst or data engineer would own in a finance or operations context: ingesting raw data, applying business logic in Python, ensuring data quality, loading to a relational database, and delivering analysis-ready SQL outputs. It reflects direct experience with AR reporting concepts gained from working in healthcare and financial services environments.
  
